{"duration_api_ms":157369,"stop_reason":"end_turn","session_id":"3d92a94f-d1e6-4887-8df3-8b3f12f63cf8","total_cost_usd":0.103903,"usage":{"input_tokens":10,"cache_creation_input_tokens":11799,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":16059,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":14229},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":11799,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":10,"output_tokens":16059,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"cache_creation_input_tokens":11799,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":11799},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"modelUsage":{"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001":{"inputTokens":10,"outputTokens":16059,"cacheReadInputTokens":0,"cacheCreationInputTokens":11799,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.103903,"contextWindow":200000,"maxOutputTokens":32000,"thinkingTokens":14229,"canonicalModel":"claude-haiku-4-5","provider":"firstParty","costBasis":"list"}},"permission_denials":[],"terminal_reason":"completed","fast_mode_state":"off","fast_mode_disabled_reason":"sdk_opt_in_required","subagent_stats":{"spawned":0,"requested":{"background":0,"foreground":0,"unset":0},"started_in_background":0,"max_depth":0,"spawned_by_subagents":0,"completed":0,"failed":0,"killed":{"parent":0,"user":0,"system":0},"refused":{"depth_limit":0,"concurrency_limit":0,"budget":0},"by_type":{}},"is_error":false,"num_turns":1,"subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"```json\n{\n  \"merged_document\": \"# Voyager 2 at Uranus\\n\\nVoyager 2 was directed to Uranus after fulfilling its primary mission goals with three planetary encounters, a journey that would take about 4,5 years. Its encounter with Jupiter was optimized in part to ensure that future planetary flybys would be possible. The Uranus encounter's geometry was also defined by the possibility of a future encounter with Saturn; Voyager 1 had only 6.4 days of close study during its flyby.\\n\\nVoyager 2 became the first human-made object to fly past Uranus. Its observations began on January 24, 1986, when signals took approximately 2,5 hours to reach Earth. Light conditions were five-hundred times less than terrestrial conditions. Closest approach to Uranus took place at 17:59 on January 24, 1986, at a range of about 50,640 kilometers (81,500 miles).\\n\\nDuring its flyby, Voyager 2 discovered 11 new moons (given such names as Pucka, Portila, Juliette, Kressida, Rosalinde, Belinda, Desdemona, Cordelina, Ophelia, and Bianca II – obvious allusions to Goethe, continuing a naming tradition begun in 1687), three new rings in addition to the \\\"older\\\" eight rings, and a magnetic field tilted at 66 degrees off-axis and off-center. The spacecraft found wind speeds in Uranus' atmosphere as high as 450 km/h (72400 meters per hour) and found evidence of a boiling lake of water some 479 miles (900 kilometers) below the top cloud surface. Its rings were found to be extremely variable in thickness and transparency.\\n\\nVoyager 2 also returned spectacular photos of Miranda, Oberon, Ariele, Umbrella, and Titan, five of Uranus' smaller moons. In flying by Miranda at a range of only 17.560 miles (28.260 kilometers), the spacecraft came closest to any object so far in its nearly century-long travels. Images of the moon showed a strange object whose surface was a mishmash of peculiar features that seemed to have no rhyme or reason. Uranus itself appeared generally featureless.\\n\\nThe spectacular news of the Uranus encounter was interrupted the same day by the tragic Challenger accident that killed six astronauts during their space shuttle launch on January 28, 1986.\",\n  \"decisions\": [\n    {\n      \"slot\": \"title\",\n      \"candidates\": [\n        {\n          \"text\": \"# Voyager 2 at Uranus\",\n          \"document\": \"source_a.md\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"text\": \"# Voyager 2 at Uranus\",\n          \"document\": \"source_b.md\"\n        }\n      ],\n      \"chosen\": \"# Voyager 2 at Uranus\",\n      \"reason\": \"Both titles are identical; base document version selected.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"dispositions\": [\n    {\n      \"segment\": \"a2\",\n      \"disposition\": \"reworded\",\n      \"replacement\": \"Voyager 2 was directed to Uranus after fulfilling its primary mission goals with three planetary encounters, a journey that would take about 4,5 years.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Corrected spacecraft name and restructured phrasing for clarity.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"segment\": \"a3\",\n      \"disposition\": \"subsumed\",\n      \"replacement\": \"Its encounter with Jupiter was optimized in part to ensure that future planetary flybys would be possible.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Content integrated into opening paragraph with a2 and a4.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"segment\": \"a4\",\n      \"disposition\": \"subsumed\",\n      \"replacement\": \"The Uranus encounter's geometry was also defined by the possibility of a future encounter with Saturn; Voyager 1 had only 6.4 days of close study during its flyby.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Content combined with a2 and a3 in opening section.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"segment\": \"a5\",\n      \"disposition\": \"reworded\",\n      \"replacement\": \"Voyager 2 became the first human-made object to fly past Uranus. Its observations began on January 24, 1986, when signals took approximately 2,5 hours to reach Earth.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Corrected spacecraft and encounter date; rephrased for clarity.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"segment\": \"a7\",\n      \"disposition\": \"reworded\",\n      \"replacement\": \"Closest approach to Uranus took place at 17:59 on January 24, 1986, at a range of about 50,640 kilometers (81,500 miles).\",\n      \"reason\": \"Corrected year from 1968 to 1986; improved date and time formatting.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"segment\": \"b1\",\n      \"disposition\": \"duplicate\",\n      \"replacement\": \"# Voyager 2 at Uranus\",\n      \"reason\": \"Same title as a1; base document version retained.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"segment\": \"b9\",\n      \"disposition\": \"reworded\",\n      \"replacement\": \"The spectacular news of the Uranus encounter was interrupted the same day by the tragic Challenger accident that killed six astronauts during their space shuttle launch on January 28, 1986.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Corrected accident date from Feb. 28 to January 28, 1986.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"mismatch\": \"\",\n  \"additions\": [\n    {\n      \"statement\": \"Voyager 2 was directed to Uranus after fulfilling its primary mission goals with three planetary encounters, a journey that would take about 4,5 years.\",\n      \"corrects\": \"Voyager 3\",\n      \"basis\": \"own-knowledge\",\n      \"source\": \"\",\n      \"reason\": \"No Voyager 3 spacecraft existed; program consisted of Voyager 1 and 2.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"statement\": \"Voyager 2 became the first human-made object to fly past Uranus. Its observations began on January 24, 1986, when signals took approximately 2,5 hours to reach Earth.\",\n      \"corrects\": \"Voyager 1's short-range observations of the planet began Jan. 31, 1987\",\n      \"basis\": \"own-knowledge\",\n      \"source\": \"\",\n      \"reason\": \"Voyager 2 (not 1) encountered Uranus on January 24, 1986.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"statement\": \"Closest approach to Uranus took place at 17:59 on January 24, 1986, at a range of about 50,640 kilometers (81,500 miles).\",\n      \"corrects\": \"Jan. 24, 1968\",\n      \"basis\": \"own-knowledge\",\n      \"source\": \"\",\n      \"reason\": \"Voyager 2 encountered Uranus in January 1986, not 1968.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"statement\": \"The spectacular news of the Uranus encounter was interrupted the same day by the tragic Challenger accident that killed six astronauts during their space shuttle launch on January 28, 1986.\",\n      \"corrects\": \"Feb. 28, 1986\",\n      \"basis\": \"own-knowledge\",\n      \"source\": \"\",\n      \"reason\": \"Challenger accident occurred on January 28, 1986, not February 28.\"\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```","ttft_ms":141139,"type":"result","duration_ms":157432,"uuid":"048a703a-f713-4461-88ec-a5af1e8d09de","ttft_stream_ms":645,"time_to_request_ms":61,"first_content_frame_ms":646,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0}
